+Reviewed DERIVATION.md SHA8ab508377035ab60321889c8f2ae44f3efc308fcc146aac8f0505549099986f3 independently before opening the native sharper proof or root's review of that proof. Verdict: PASS for the declared supplied compressed-source operator family and simultaneous schedule. No missing k loss or counterexample was found. Nine exact scalar controls are retained in check.py/result.json; they are supporting arithmetic, not a numerical certification of analytic inequalities.
+
+## Uniform action and cutoff bounds
+
+The nonnegative deficits give E_k>=E_1 and Hessian Q_k>=Q_1 for every k>=1. A single compact product chart and single complement gap therefore suffice. Derivatives of the finite analytic action words are bounded by Ck through the required orders. The Hessian-integral formula differentiates at fixed z and does not require epsilon*k small: its first and second derivatives cost k|z|³ and k|z|⁴. Differentiating the exponential adds the square of the first derivative, hence k² times a fixed polynomial, dominated by exp(-c|z|²) uniformly. Cutoff derivatives remain inside the same quadratic chart. At zero the first derivative is the actual cubic, whose nuisance integral vanishes for every fixed source pair by the reviewed color-isotropic two-source identity. Thus both full partition and unnormalized marginal remainders cost Ck²/beta. No k-dependent Gaussian peak has been replaced by a constant here.
+
+The determinant has degree12 with bounds k^12<=detH_k<=(55/2)k^12 for k>=1. Eight colors turn its Gaussian partition into a quantity comparable to k^-48. The absolute partition remainder Ck²/beta becomes a small relative error under beta>=Ck50, giving the claimed lower bound. The compact tail beta68 exp(-c beta) can be bounded by C/beta uniformly, independently of k, using E_k>=E_1.
+
+## Fiber tails and normalization
+
+The unnormalized Gaussian marginal M0 is uniformly bounded by integrating exp(-Q1) over nuisance variables. This is legitimate even though the normalized p0 peak grows. Outside the fixed nuisance chart, the actual scaled marginal costs beta60 exp(-c beta); inside the source chart R²<=C_chart beta. Splitting the gap exponential and choosing a sufficiently weak fixed Gaussian in R absorbs both the radius and every beta polynomial. The same argument for the Gaussian follows from Q_k>=Q_1. These are pointwise fiber estimates, not just total probability tails.
+
+The identity p-p0=(M-M0)/D+M0(D0-D)/(DD0) gives respectively k50/beta and k98/beta. Both inverse partitions have correctly been charged in this deliberately crude form. Multiplying by bounded reciprocal source half-Haar factors costs no additional k. Their Taylor difference costs R²/beta times p0, bounded by Ck48 exp(-cR²), which is dominated by the advertised k98 bound. The expanding-chart Gaussian tail is likewise controlled by k48 times a fixed Gaussian. All fixed polynomial-Gaussian envelopes are integrable in L1 and L2. Angular averaging and subsequent oscillator coordinate changes are contractions/unitaries and introduce no new power of k.
+
+## Operator normalization and powers
+
+The off-chart full group kernel bound C beta68 k48 exp(-c beta) yields the scaled Hilbert-Schmidt estimate C beta64 k48 exp(-c beta), since the original Haar domain has finite normalized measure. This is bounded by Ck98/beta. Consequently the actual scaled norm, not merely the chart norm, differs from lambda_k by that amount. The explicit formula for lambda_k is uniformly positive and bounded for k>=1; its k=1 value agrees exactly with the earlier absolute normalization. Requiring beta>=Ck98 makes inversion of the actual norm uniformly harmless.
+
+C=A/||A|| and its compression are contractions. The chart partial isometry satisfies U*U=P; it does not identify the complement with a false fixed-group continuum limit. For the actual powers, the noncommuting telescoping identity bounds ||C^n-(PCP)^n|| by n||C-PCP||. This and the telescope to the oscillator contraction both cost O(n k98/beta). At k_n=4n²/(5t²), beta_n=n200, this is O(n^-3), and the normalization threshold holds eventually. The explicit rational r_k expansion gives n t_k_n=t+O(n^-2). The full nonnegative number-operator spectrum then gives O(n^-2) operator-norm time error. No finite-mode truncation or hidden uniform spectral gap is used.
+
+The proof explicitly and correctly distinguishes powers of the compressed source operator from compression of a longer full transfer slab. Its reset/composition rule, anisotropy/time schedule, fixed geometry and Wilson/Haar model remain supplied. Constants and onset can depend on fixed t; no usable finite-resource accuracy or physical elapsed-time claim follows. The OU coefficients and inherited boundary-domain interpretation agree with the separately reviewed fixed-anisotropy proof.
